course: dictionary
lesson: 12083
title: Closest: the nearest of all
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Closest means the nearest of all. When you compare three or more things, closest tells you which one is least far away. «The closest shop is just round the corner.» Of all the shops, this one is the nearest to you.
explain: Closest can also be about amounts. In maths, the closest number is the one with the smallest difference. 48 is closest to 50, because it is only 2 away, while 40 is 8 away.
explain: Look at the -est ending: close, closer, closest. Close means near, closer means nearer than something else, and closest means the nearest of all. «Dan's closest friend lives next door» means that of all his friends, the one he is nearest to lives next door.
explain: Safety matters too. «Find the closest fire exit as soon as you walk into a room.» To check the meaning, swap in nearest. If the sentence still makes sense, you have understood closest.
fact: Closest means the nearest of all.
fact: The -est ending shows the most or the least: close, closer, closest.
fact: Closest can mean the nearest in amount, the number with the smallest difference.
fact: «A closest friend» is the friend you are nearest to.
fact: A good check is to swap in nearest. If it still makes sense, closest is the right meaning.
exercise: «The closest shop is just round the corner.» What does closest mean here? | *The nearest of all | The most expensive | The oldest | The biggest
exercise: Which number is closest to 48? | 30 | *50 | 60 | 40
exercise: Copy the word that means the nearest of all: «Maya sat closest to the door.» | *closest | sat | door | to
test: «Dan's closest friend lives next door.» What does closest mean? | The newest | The loudest | *The nearest of all
test: Which number is closest to 19? | 30 | 10 | *20
exam: Which is a good way to check the meaning of closest? | Skip it and never come back to it | Look only at the longest word in the sentence | Guess without reading the rest | *Swap in nearest and see if the sentence still makes sense
